  "Question": "How many integer solutions of the inequality \\(x^2-4>0\\) have an absolute value less than 4?",
  "Choices": {},
  "Correct Answer": "2",
  "Accepted Answers": [],
  "Unordered Answers": false,
  "Solution": "\\(x^2>4\\) gives \\(x<-2\\) or \\(x>2\\). With the additional restriction \\(-4<x<4\\), the only integer solutions are \\(-3\\) and 3, giving 2 in total.\n\nAlternative method 1: Check the seven possible integers\n\nThe condition \\(|x|<4\\) restricts the integer candidates to \\(-3,-2,-1,0,1,2,3\\). Substituting them into \\(x^2-4\\) gives \\(5,0,-3,-4,-3,0,5\\), respectively. Only the values at \\(x=-3\\) and \\(x=3\\) are positive, so there are \\(2\\) solutions.",
